var EXP=["Present Simple does not mean <b>now</b>. It describes what is generally true: your routine, your job, facts about the world. That is why we say <b>I work in a bank</b> even when we are at the cinema. Use it with time words like <b>every day</b>, <b>usually</b> or <b>never</b>. The one thing to remember is the <b>-s</b> for he, she and it. In questions and negatives, <b>do</b> or <b>does</b> carries that -s, so the main verb stays simple: <b>Does she work?</b>",
"Present Continuous is about <b>the moment</b>. It describes actions in progress right now, temporary situations like <b>I am staying with a friend this week</b>, and plans already arranged like <b>We are meeting at six</b>. Build it with <b>am, is or are</b> plus a verb ending in <b>-ing</b>. Be careful with state verbs such as know, like, want and love. They describe states, not actions, so we say <b>I know</b>, not <b>I am knowing</b>.",
"Present Perfect <b>connects the past to the present</b>. You do not say when something happened. You care about the result or the experience. <b>I have lost my keys</b> means I still have a problem today. Compare <b>I lost my keys yesterday</b>, which is just a past event. Use <b>have or has</b> plus the past participle (been, done, seen, worked). Words like already, yet, just, ever and never fit well, and <b>for</b> and <b>since</b> show how long.",
"Present Perfect Continuous focuses on the <b>activity and how long</b> it has lasted, up to now. <b>I have been learning English since January</b> shows an ongoing process. It also explains a present result: <b>My hands are dirty because I have been gardening</b>. Compare it with Present Perfect: <b>I have read three chapters</b> counts the result, while <b>I have been reading</b> describes the activity. Build it with <b>have or has + been + -ing</b>.",
"Past Simple is the tense for <b>finished stories</b>. You know when it happened, or the time is clear: yesterday, last week, in 2019. Regular verbs end in <b>-ed</b>, and many common verbs are irregular and must be learned (go, went; see, saw). In questions and negatives, <b>did</b> carries the past, so the main verb goes back to its base form: <b>Did you go?</b> and <b>I didn't go</b>. Use it to tell stories in order.",
"Past Continuous <b>sets the scene</b>. It describes an action that was in progress at a moment in the past, or the background to a main event. <b>I was cooking when you called</b> gives a long action (cooking) and a shorter one that interrupted it (called). Use <b>was or were + -ing</b>. It is perfect for storytelling, because it paints the situation, while Past Simple moves the story forward.",
"Past Perfect shows <b>which of two past events happened first</b>. <b>When I arrived, the train had left</b> means the train left before I arrived. Without it, the order can be unclear. Build it with <b>had + past participle</b>, the same for every subject. You will often see it with <b>before, after, by the time</b> and <b>already</b>. If the order is obvious from the words first and then, Past Simple is often enough.",
"Past Perfect Continuous looks back from a past moment at an <b>activity that had been going on</b> for some time, often to explain a past situation. <b>She was tired because she had been working all night.</b> Build it with <b>had been + -ing</b>. It is less common than other tenses, but it is very useful for reasons, and for duration before a past point, with <b>for</b> and <b>since</b>.",
"Future Simple with <b>will</b> is for decisions made at the moment of speaking, predictions, promises and offers. <b>I'll help you</b> is an offer, and <b>It will rain</b> is a prediction. The form is easy: <b>will + base verb</b>, for every subject. In speech we usually say <b>'ll</b>. It is not the only way to talk about the future, because plans already decided often use <b>going to</b> or the present continuous, but will is the most flexible.",
"Future Continuous pictures an <b>action in progress at a particular time in the future</b>: <b>This time tomorrow I will be flying.</b> It is also a polite way to ask about plans: <b>Will you be using the car tonight?</b> Build it with <b>will be + -ing</b>. Think of putting a pin in the future and looking at what is happening at that moment.",
"Future Perfect looks back from a future point and says something will be <b>complete by then</b>. <b>By Friday I will have finished the report</b> means the work is done on or before Friday. Build it with <b>will have + past participle</b>. The key word is <b>by</b>: by Friday, by then, by the time you arrive. It is common in plans, deadlines and predictions.",
"Future Perfect Continuous is the rarest tense, but it is handy for <b>anniversaries and long processes</b>. <b>By June, I will have been working here for ten years</b> stresses how long something will have lasted by a future point. Build it with <b>will have been + -ing</b>, and expect <b>for</b> and <b>by</b> in the sentence. If it feels heavy, remember it simply adds duration to Future Perfect."];
